## Metadata
Copyright: New York World
Date: 1914-07-19
Number: 28
Notes: Headed "FUN'S UNCOMPLETED CROSS-WORD PUZZLE"; the number is printed as "No 28." in the centre block. A 13-wide diamond with that diamond-shaped panel covering a stepped void in the centre (shown as _), no numbers and no clue list; the solver fills in the blank squares around letters already printed in the grid. Letters printed as given: r3c5-c9 GAOLS; r5c3-c6 GATE; r5c8-c11 DRAW; r6c3 A; r6c9-c12 ENOS; r7c1 H; r7c3 S; r7c13 P; r8c3 E; r9c3 S; r9c6 T; r9c8-c11 WELD; r10c6 U; r10c7 S; r11c5-c9 FREAK; r12c6 N; r12c7 E; r13c7 N. Two givens disagree with the printed solution: r7c1 is printed H but the solution has W (WASH), and r13c7 is printed N but the solution has R (SEER). The grid here follows the solution. Printed instructions: "Today's cross word puzzle offers still another variation in the series. Instead of solving the puzzle in the usual way see if you can first complete it by filling in the blank squares with letters so as to spell words when read either across or up and down. You do not require a numbered list of clews and definitions to work out this puzzle. Just think of a house and the furniture, fittings and supplies you will find in a house. With this clew to work on, you can readily call to mind nearly all the words needed to complete the puzzle. The solution, or rather the completed puzzle, will appear in next week's FUN." No credit is printed. Read by hand from the Milwaukee Sentinel's page (Milwaukee_Sentinel_1914_07_19_79.png), solution from Milwaukee_Sentinel_1914_07_26_76.png, by Claude, 2026-11-16.
Numbering: none


